--TEST--
A property write through an `object` or `mixed` receiver into an anonymous object stores only a value with the shape of the field's current value, so a typed read never sees an object of the wrong class — `rule:types/erased-member-access`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// An anonymous object has no declared class. Each write below goes through an
// `object` or a `mixed` variable, so the program checks the value when it runs.
// The new value must have the type of the value the field has now.
class Animal { public string $name = "tom"; }
class Dog extends Animal { public int $legs = 4; }
class Puppy extends Dog {}

type Narrow = {pet: Dog, pets: array<Dog>, home: {pet: Dog}, tag: ?string};
// A write through `object` or `mixed` does not take a `mixed` value, so `Value` lists every value below.
type Value = ?(string|Animal|array<Animal>|{pet: Animal});

class Write {
    public static function throughObject(object $o, Value $value): string {
        try {
            $o->pet = $value;
            return "written";
        } catch (RuntimeError $e) {
            return $e->message;
        }
    }

    public static function throughMixed(mixed $m, string $field, Value $value): string {
        try {
            if ($field == "pet") { $m->pet = $value; }
            if ($field == "pets") { $m->pets = $value; }
            if ($field == "home") { $m->home = $value; }
            if ($field == "tag") { $m->tag = $value; }
            return "written";
        } catch (RuntimeError $e) {
            return $e->message;
        }
    }
}

Narrow $n = {pet: new Dog(), pets: [new Dog()], home: {pet: new Dog()}, tag: null};
echo Write::throughObject($n, new Puppy()), "\n";
echo Write::throughObject($n, new Animal()), "\n";
echo Write::throughMixed($n, "pet", new Animal()), "\n";
echo Write::throughMixed($n, "pet", null), "\n";
echo Write::throughMixed($n, "pets", [new Dog(), new Puppy()]), "\n";
echo Write::throughMixed($n, "pets", [new Animal()]), "\n";
echo Write::throughMixed($n, "home", {pet: new Puppy()}), "\n";
echo Write::throughMixed($n, "home", {pet: new Animal()}), "\n";
echo Write::throughMixed($n, "tag", null), "\n";
echo Write::throughMixed($n, "tag", "a"), "\n";

// The refused writes stored nothing, so every typed read finds a `Dog`.
echo $n->pet->legs, " ", $n->pets[1]->legs, " ", $n->home->pet->legs, "\n";
--EXPECT--
written
field `pet` of an anonymous object is an object of class `Puppy`, so an object of class `Animal` cannot be written to it
field `pet` of an anonymous object is an object of class `Puppy`, so an object of class `Animal` cannot be written to it
field `pet` of an anonymous object has the type `object`, so `null` cannot be written to it
written
field `pets` of an anonymous object is an array, so an array holding an object of class `Animal` cannot be written to it
written
field `home` of an anonymous object is an anonymous object, so an object whose `pet` is an object of class `Animal` cannot be written to it
written
field `tag` of an anonymous object is `null`, so a string cannot be written to it
4 4 4
